# IYDT-GSRP optical analyses

These decks infer SUGAR log-yield and HOB from two optical response channels.
Each assessment has a fixed-benchmark-yield version and an errors-in-variables
(EIV) version that infers selected benchmark yields.

## Native prerequisite

Create the optical prior-code link from this directory:

```text
ln -s ../../../Applications/Code/IYDT-gsrp/Phenomenology Code
```

## Analyses

| Directory | Benchmark yield | Type |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `I-EIV-SUGAR-hob-0` | EIV | rapid calibration + event |
| `I-SUGAR-hob-0` | fixed | rapid calibration + event |
| `I-EIV-SUGAR-hob` | EIV | complete joint assessment |
| `I-SUGAR-hob` | fixed | complete joint assessment |

For a rapid case, run `runMPEM.r` in the background, wait for successful
completion, and always preserve its pristine `.RData` separately (for example,
as `.RData-calibration`) for future event analyses. Then run `runMPEM_0.r` in
the background. Complete cases run only `runMPEM.r`.

Each calibration/complete deck writes `opt.RData`. MultiPEM EIV cases require
the EIV result; fixed-yield cases require the fixed-yield result. Follow the
exact destination names in `../2-Phen-oc/README` or `../4-Phen/README`.

Docker analysis names begin `IYDT-gsrp/Optical/`. Docker creates the local prior
directory in its snapshot and checkpoints rapid calibration. Optimization
results still have to be staged into canonical MultiPEM `Opt` inputs before a
dependent Docker job is created.

EIV is a model choice with scientific consequences. Verify the selected
directory, `eiv` settings, prior configuration, sampler, worker counts, and
seeds before running.
